<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope Gregory IX</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1241">1241</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Ugolino di Conti</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gregory IX was pope from 1227 to 1241, a relative of Innocent III. He repeatedly excommunicated Emperor Frederick II and fought him for control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and from 1231 he appointed papal inquisitors to pursue heresy. He canonised Francis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assisi">Assisi</a> and Dominic, and in 1234 issued the Decretals, a major collection of canon law.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-ix.jpg" alt="Pope Gregory IX" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francesco Bini · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ritratto_di_gregorio_ix,_dalla_facciata_dell%27antica_basilica_di_s._pietro,_1225-50_ca._(museo_di_roma)_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ugolino dei Conti was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anagni">Anagni</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Sources give his birth between about 1145 and 1170, and he is said to have been in his nineties, if not nearly a hundred, at his death. He studied at the Universities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a>. His cousin Innocent III made him Cardinal-Deacon of Sant'Eustachio in December 1198, and in 1206 he became Cardinal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Ostia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/velletri">Velletri</a>. He was Dean of the Sacred College of Cardinals by 1218 or 1219. At the request of Francis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assisi">Assisi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-honorius-iii">Pope Honorius III</a> appointed him Cardinal Protector of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a> in 1220.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Elected pope in 1227, he took the name Gregory because he assumed the office at the monastery of Saint Gregory ad Septem Solia. He continued the policy of papal supremacy of Gregory VII and Innocent III. In 1231 his bull Parens scientiarum, issued after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-paris">University of Paris</a> strike of 1229, settled differences between the scholars and the local authorities by extending papal patronage; it has been called the university's magna carta. He also proclaimed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> against the Stedinger in October 1232 and endorsed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-crusades">Northern Crusades</a>.</p><p>Gregory began his pontificate by suspending Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a> for delay in carrying out the promised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixth-crusade">Sixth Crusade</a>, and excommunication followed. Frederick took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, but Gregory distrusted him, and the war of 1228 to 1230 is known as the War of the Keys. After a truce, Frederick's defeat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombard-league">Lombard League</a> in 1239 raised the prospect that he would dominate all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and Gregory excommunicated him again and denounced him as a heretic. The struggle ended only with Gregory's death on 22 August 1241; his successor Innocent IV declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> in 1245.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Gregory had Nova Compilatio decretalium, the Decretals, prepared and promulgated in 1234, completing the systematising of papal pronouncements begun with Gratian's Decretum of 1140. It gave papal legal theory its foundation. It also gave the doctrine of the perpetual servitude of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> the force of canon law. In 1239 he ordered all copies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Talmud</a> confiscated, after the intervention of the convert Nicholas Donin.</p><p>He appointed papal inquisitors, mostly Dominicans and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a>, from 1231. Thomas Madden holds that the aim was to bring due process to the haphazard episcopal inquisitions; Walter Ullmann judged the procedure a systematic perversion of justice. Gregory supported the mendicant orders, befriended Dominic and Clare of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assisi">Assisi</a>, and canonised Elisabeth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, Dominic, Anthony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a> and Francis of Assisi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pope Gregory IX” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Salza secured the Golden Bull of Rimini, dated 1226 though possibly issued later, in which Emperor Frederick II granted the Order the Kulmerland (Chełmno Land) and whatever Prussian territory it conquered, and in 1234 <b>Pope Gregory IX</b> took these lands under papal protection by the Bull of Rieti.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-the-teutonic-order">State of the Teutonic Order · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Pope Gregory IX</b>, on the advice of the Dominican Raymond of Penyafort, introduced it in the diocese of Rome on Fridays at Compline, and many dioceses and orders took it up during the 13th century.</blockquote><a href="/songs/salve-regina">Salve Regina</a></li><li><blockquote>With <b>Pope Gregory IX</b>'s permission, the episcopal seat moved from Nousiainen to Koroinen, near the centre of present-day Turku.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-23">23 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Following the death of Pope Honorius III, <b>Ugolino di Conti</b> was elected pope and took the name Gregory IX.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-19">19 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Pope Gregory IX</b> issued the bull Rex pacificus for a new collection of canon law, compiled by the Dominican Raymond of Peñafort to replace the Decretum Gratiani.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-5">5 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Emperor Frederick II arrived at Acre and was warmly received by the Templars, Hospitallers and clergy, but, being excommunicated by <b>Pope Gregory IX</b>, was denied the kiss of peace.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-7">7 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anagni">Anagni</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.75,13.15&amp;year=1231" aria-label="Anagni on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4870 676 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pope Gregory IX"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5320 946h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5320 946h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.75,13.15&amp;year=1231">What was here in 1231? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
